  "worked_solution": "Since we are given that ${a}_{4}=2\\,304$ and ${a}_{6}=36\\,864$, we can find the common ratio $r$ of the sequence, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{n-m} & =\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}} \\\\ {r}^{6-4} & =\\frac{{a}_{6}}{{a}_{4}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{36\\,864}{2\\,304} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =16 \\\\ r & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since ${a}_{4}=2\\,304$ and  $r=4$, we can use the $n$th term formula to solve for ${a}_{1}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\ {a}_{4} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {4}^{4-1} \\\\ 2\\,304 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {4}^{3} \\\\ 2\\,304 & =64{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the geometric sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =36\\cdot {4}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want $n$th term to be greater than $496\\,800.$\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}36\\cdot {4}^{n-1} & >496\\,800 \\\\ {4}^{n-1} & >13\\,800 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}\u2061({4}^{n-1}) & >\\operatorname{log}\u2061(13\\,800) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}\u2061(4) & >\\operatorname{log}\u2061(13\\,800) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}\u2061(13\\,800)}{\\operatorname{log}\u2061(4)} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}\u2061(13\\,800)}{\\operatorname{log}\u2061(4)}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}\u2061(13\\,800)}{\\operatorname{log}\u2061(4)}+1\u22487.876.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $8$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $496\\,800.$",
